    <section id="canonical-abi" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>02.3 · component mechanics</span>
        <h2>A WIT string is not a shared <code>&amp;str</code>.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="canonical-grid">
        <div class="canonical-copy">
          <p>WIT presents <code>string</code> as a language-level value. At a linear-memory core boundary, the canonical ABI lowers that value to a representation involving an offset and a length, allocates with the component’s canonical reallocator when necessary, and validates bounds and encoding while lifting the result.</p>
          <p>Generated bindings hide those details, which is the point: host Rust receives an owned <code>String</code>; guest Rust receives its own value in guest-managed memory. Neither side receives a native pointer into the other side’s ordinary heap.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="memory-cutaway">
          <div class="memory-space host-memory"><span>host address space</span><strong>Rust heap</strong><code>String { ptr, len, cap }</code></div>
          <div class="canonical-gate"><span>lower</span><strong>canonical ABI</strong><span>lift</span></div>
          <div class="memory-space guest-memory"><span>Store-owned linear memory</span><strong>guest bytes</strong><code>offset + length</code></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="invariant-notes">
        <article><span>bounds</span><h3>Offsets are checked</h3><p>A guest-supplied offset/length pair must describe in-bounds memory. An invalid canonical value traps rather than becoming a Rust reference.</p></article>
        <article><span>borrow</span><h3>Store access is exclusive</h3><p>Host memory views are tied to access to the Store. Growing memory can invalidate prior views, so long-lived raw slices are the wrong abstraction.</p></article>
        <article><span>lifetime</span><h3>Current memory dies per call</h3><p>Dekopon creates a fresh Store and instance for each operation. No guest pointer remains meaningful on the next invocation.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="strategies" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>02.4 · design space</span>
        <h2>Serialization is a product decision, not only a speed decision.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table strategy-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Strategy</th><th scope="col">Boundary shape</th><th scope="col">Strength</th><th scope="col">Cost / risk</th><th scope="col">Dekopon fit</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">JSON over WIT strings</th><td><code>string → string</code></td><td>Easy inspection, easy version-zero iteration, natural LLM/tool interop.</td><td>Repeated UTF-8 encoding, parsing, allocation, and runtime-only field typing.</td><td><span class="table-status current">Current</span> Small control payloads while the trait stabilizes.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Typed WIT records / variants</th><td><code>record</code>, <code>result</code>, <code>variant</code></td><td>Language-neutral static contract; generated validation; removes the inner JSON envelope.</td><td>Contract changes need explicit versioning; recursive/dynamic JSON remains awkward; strings/lists still lower through memory.</td><td><span class="table-status explore">Candidate</span> Strong destination for stable manifests and responses.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">CBOR / MessagePack / Protobuf bytes</th><td><code>list&lt;u8&gt;</code></td><td>Compact binary payloads; schemaful options can be efficient and deterministic.</td><td>Still copies bytes; adds codec/tooling complexity; poor browser/operator readability; LLM edge is still JSON-like.</td><td><span class="table-status explore">Situational</span> Useful only after measured payload pressure.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">WIT resources / handles</th><td>opaque typed handle</td><td>Keeps large or sensitive state host-owned; operations can stream or page data through narrow methods.</td><td>More host calls, lifecycle rules, revocation, and TOCTOU concerns; creates an authority-bearing interface.</td><td><span class="table-status direction">Future tool</span> Better than handing credentials or broad raw memory to a provider.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Raw shared linear memory protocol</th><td>offsets, lengths, ring buffers</td><td>Can reduce copies for high-volume local data when ownership is rigorously designed.</td><td>Manual ABI, synchronization, aliasing, bounds, lifetime, portability, and sandbox-review burden.</td><td><span class="table-status avoid">Not a default</span> Requires benchmark proof and a narrow threat model.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="zero-copy-note">
        <span>About “zero-copy” codecs</span>
        <h3>Zero-copy decoding is not automatically zero-copy crossing.</h3>
        <p>Offset-based formats such as FlatBuffers or Cap’n Proto can let a guest traverse a validated buffer without first rebuilding a tree of objects. The buffer still has to arrive in that guest’s linear memory unless the architecture also defines shared memory or host-backed access. Rust-specific archived layouts such as <code>rkyv</code> add alignment, validation, versioning, and language-portability questions; they are not a drop-in replacement for a WIT contract.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="article-callout callout-decision">
        <strong>Likely optimization order</strong>
        <p>Measure first. Stabilize high-value shapes as typed WIT. Avoid serializing the same logical value twice. Introduce resource handles or streams for genuinely large host-owned data. Reach for a custom shared-memory protocol only if profiling shows canonical typed transfers cannot meet a concrete budget.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="direct-memory" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>02.5 · the tempting shortcut</span>
        <h2>What “direct memory sharing” can—and cannot—mean.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="memory-myths">
        <article>
          <span class="myth-label">Not possible as stated</span>
          <h3>“Pass a Rust struct pointer into Wasm.”</h3>
          <p>A native Rust pointer points into host virtual memory and follows Rust layout and lifetime rules. A Wasm32 pointer is an offset in one instance’s linear memory. They are not interchangeable addresses.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="myth-label possible">Possible, lower level</span>
          <h3>“Let the host read or write guest linear memory.”</h3>
          <p>Wasmtime can expose an instance’s linear memory to the embedder while the Store is correctly borrowed. A custom core-Wasm ABI can use offsets and lengths. This bypasses much component-model ergonomics, not the need for validation and ownership.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="myth-label possible">Possible, specialized</span>
          <h3>“Use a shared Wasm memory or ring buffer.”</h3>
          <p>WebAssembly shared-memory/thread features can support concurrent protocols where runtime, target, and component choices allow it. Atomics, cancellation, backpressure, and malicious indices become part of the security design.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="myth-label preferred">Often preferable</span>
          <h3>“Keep data in the host and pass a handle.”</h3>
          <p>A typed resource can represent a host-owned buffer, response body, or scoped client. The guest gets only methods the linker exposes; the host keeps credentials, destinations, and lifetime enforcement on its side.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p>For Dekopon, direct access is especially sensitive because a future broker host will own authority. A fast buffer path must not become a side channel around output limits, evidence capture, destination constraints, or credential isolation. Zero-copy is not zero-policy.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="llm-edge" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>02.6 · outside the process</span>
        <h2>Shared memory stops at the model boundary.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="llm-edge-board">
        <div><span>local process</span><strong>Rust conversation state</strong><code>Vec&lt;ModelMessage&gt;</code></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">serialize request →</i>
        <div class="llm-edge-wire"><span>network</span><strong>HTTP body / SSE stream</strong><code>JSON-compatible protocol</code></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→ parse response</i>
        <div><span>another process or machine</span><strong>model server</strong><code>Ollama-compatible or ChatGPT/Codex</code></div>
      </div>

      <p>An in-process memory optimization can reduce the Rust↔Wasm portion only. The prompt, tool definitions, model-generated arguments, tool results, and final response still cross an HTTP protocol boundary. For remote models they also cross a machine and trust boundary. That usually makes clear contracts, bounds, and redaction more valuable than shaving a small control-message copy.</p>

